<Window x:Class="Bws.Gui.MainWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="{DynamicResource gui.window.title}"
        Background="{DynamicResource ApplicationBackgroundBrush}"
        UseLayoutRounding="True"
        SnapsToDevicePixels="True"
        TextOptions.TextFormattingMode="Display"
        TextOptions.TextRenderingMode="ClearType"
        Height="700" Width="1100">

    <!--
        Every appearance value here points at a name in Themes/Values.xaml, and a guard in the
        architecture tests fails the build on one written in place - ADR-23. Height and Width
        are the window's own size, which is a one-off rather than something two screens can
        disagree about, so they are not policed.

        Text comes from the language file the same way, by key. A column header cannot bind
        to the view model: columns are not in the visual tree and inherit no data context,
        which is the trap this markup fell into on the first try.

        Background is set here rather than left alone, and it is a condition rather than a
        touch of polish: WPF UI's dictionaries do not give a plain Window a background, so
        without this line the window comes up white and THE WHOLE LIST DISAPPEARS - light text
        on a light surface - while the status line still reports that 811 entries were read.
        docs/10-WPF-UI.md, trap 2.

        THE FOUR RENDERING SETTINGS ARE MEASURED, NOT COPIED FROM AN ANSWER ON THE INTERNET.
        They arrived as a suggestion from the owner and were checked the way anything about
        pixels has to be: two builds differing only in these lines, the same text captured by
        PrintWindow from the release build, magnified nine times and compared. With them the
        strokes are thicker, whiter and land on the pixel grid. Without them the same glyphs
        are paler and sit at fractional positions.

        Measured AS A SET. Which of the four does the work was not separated, and saying so is
        cheaper than four more builds for an answer nobody needs yet. TextFormattingMode is the
        one with a known cost: Display mode is meant for small text and can look worse for
        large. This window has none, and that stops being true the day it gets a heading.
    -->

    <Grid Margin="{StaticResource PadPanel}">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />

            <!--
                The filters, on their own row since 2026-08-11. Eight of them do not fit beside
                a search box that has to stay wide enough to read a query in, and A5 will grow
                this row rather than shrink it - account and signature are waiting on other
                slices. Auto, so the row costs nothing on a window too narrow to show it.
            -->
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />

            <RowDefinition Height="*" />
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!--
            One field for all three of A1, A2 and A3, which is the point rather than a saving:
            a person types a word, learns one operator, and never moves to a different box.
            The same text handed to the query switch of bws list picks the same entries.

            Both switches sit beside it because both are about how the text is read, and one
            of them writes into it.
        -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="0" Margin="{StaticResource MarginBelowSearch}">
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="{StaticResource WidthCountLine}" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

            <Grid Grid.Column="0" Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}">
                <!--
                    Updated on every keystroke rather than when the field loses focus. The
                    filtering is the feature, and a list that waits for Tab is a list that
                    does not filter as you type.
                -->
                <TextBox x:Name="QueryBox"
                         Text="{Binding QueryText, UpdateSourceTrigger=PropertyChanged}" />

                <!--
                    The hint, behind the field and out of the way of the mouse. An empty box
                    teaches nobody that the language exists, and this is the one place it can
                    be shown without spending a line of the window on it.
                -->
                <TextBlock Text="{DynamicResource gui.search.hint}"
                           Margin="{StaticResource PadCell}"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           IsHitTestVisible="False">
                    <TextBlock.Style>
                        <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource SubduedText}">
                            <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                            <Style.Triggers>
                                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Text, ElementName=QueryBox}" Value="">
                                    <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                                </DataTrigger>
                            </Style.Triggers>
                        </Style>
                    </TextBlock.Style>
                </TextBlock>
            </Grid>

            <!--
                THE DRIVERS CHECKBOX STOOD HERE UNTIL 2026-08-11 AND IS NOW A CHIP IN THE ROW
                BELOW. It was A5's promise arriving early - one control that read the query and
                wrote into it - and A5 arrived, so it joined the others rather than sitting
                beside them meaning the same thing in a different shape. docs/11 complaint 7
                left the grouping of this row deliberately unfinished for exactly this moment.
            -->

            <!--
                THE COUNT, AND IT MOVED UP HERE FROM UNDER THE LIST. Complaint 8 of the eleven:
                it was the least visible thing on the screen - small, grey, bottom left - and it
                is the main answer to every keystroke in the box beside it. Beside the box is
                where somebody is already looking.

                It doubles as complaint 7, "you cannot see THAT it is filtering". A narrowed list
                reads "108 of 810 entries" rather than "810 entries", which says both that a
                filter is on and how much it took - and the style below makes it semibold when
                narrowed, so the difference is visible without reading the number.

                Trimmed with a tooltip, because this line also carries the failure message, which
                is the system's and can be any length. The same failure is said again in the
                middle of the empty list, where there is room for it.
            -->
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="2"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="countLine"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       Text="{Binding Says.Status}"
                       TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                       ToolTip="{Binding Says.Status}"
                       Style="{StaticResource CountLine}" />
        </Grid>

        <!--
            THE CLICKABLE FILTERS, A5. Each one stands for one member of the query, and clicking
            it writes that member into the box above - which is the promise: the filter you
            clicked is a query you can read, edit and learn from. The tooltip shows the member
            before the click rather than after, so the box is not the only place it appears.

            AN ItemsControl RATHER THAN EIGHT CONTROLS WRITTEN OUT. The chips are data - which
            ones there are is decided in FilterChips.cs, where the reason for each is written
            beside it - and markup listing them again would be a second copy that drifts. It
            also means the row that account and signature chips will join is already built.

            WrapPanel, so a narrow window stacks them instead of clipping them. The count line
            beside the search box is the only thing in this window allowed to be cut, because it
            carries a message of the system's own length.

            NO STYLE OF OUR OWN, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN LAZY. Themes/Controls.xaml
            stood exactly on its 530-line ceiling on the day this was written, so a chip style
            there would have forced a second split of the theme inside one session. WPF UI
            already styles ToggleButton, and the only thing ours needed was the gap between
            them. If these turn out to need a look of their own, that is a slice with a seam in
            front of it rather than four lines squeezed in.
        -->
        <!--
            THE TWO AutomationId VALUES BELOW ARE ON THE CONTROLS, NOT ON THIS GRID, AND THAT IS
            A MEASURED CORRECTION. An id was put on the Grid first, and automation never saw it:
            WPF builds a peer for controls, not for layout panels, so the identifier sat on an
            element that is not in the tree at all. tools/window-journey needs to tell the labels
            of controls apart from the sentences the window says about the answer, and it does
            that by identity rather than by where things sit - the same lesson that file has now
            learnt three times.
        -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="1" Margin="{StaticResource MarginBelowSearch}">
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

            <!--
                THE GROUP LABEL COMPLAINT 7 ASKED FOR, and it waited here on purpose. docs/11
                closed that complaint in half on 2026-08-05 and said the other half - a label
                over the controls - was deliberately not built, because A5 was going to arrive
                and rewrite the row. It has, so the label arrives with the row it labels rather
                than being invented twice.

                The hint is the tooltip rather than a second line, because the sentence explains
                a mechanism somebody discovers by clicking once - it is worth having and not
                worth a line of a window whose job is 810 rows.
            -->
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="filterGroup"
                       Text="{DynamicResource gui.filter.group}"
                       ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.filter.hint}"
                       Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}" />

        <ItemsControl Grid.Column="1"
                      AutomationProperties.AutomationId="filterChips"
                      ItemsSource="{Binding Filters}">
            <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    <WrapPanel />
                </ItemsPanelTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>

            <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate>
                    <ToggleButton Margin="{StaticResource MarginChip}"
                                  Content="{Binding Label}"
                                  ToolTip="{Binding Member}"
                                  IsChecked="{Binding IsOn, Mode=TwoWay}" />
                </DataTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
        </ItemsControl>

            <!--
                WHICH COLUMNS THE LIST SHOWS, `A8`. A VISIBLE CONTROL RATHER THAN A MENU HIDDEN
                UNDER THE COLUMN HEADINGS, and that is `docs/11` rather than taste: section 6 grades
                "recognition rather than recall" as one of this window's two weakest heuristics, and
                a feature reachable only by right clicking something is a feature you have to
                already know about. The filters made the same choice one slice earlier - complaint 7
                was closed with a visible row and a group label, not with a menu.

                The list ITSELF is a menu, which is the opposite decision and is about surface
                rather than discoverability: a panel of our own would need a fill, an edge and both
                of their contrast ratios argued, for a thing somebody opens a few times. The theme
                already dresses a menu and this window already uses one to copy a name.

                It sits at the end of the filters row rather than beside the search box, because
                what it changes is the list rather than the question - and the count line, which
                answers the question, already owns the right-hand end of the row above.

                Its own AutomationId, because tools/window-journey tells the labels of controls
                apart from the sentences the window says about the answer, and it does that by
                identity. This is the fourth time that lesson has been paid for in this file.
            -->
            <Button Grid.Column="2"
                    x:Name="ColumnsButton"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="columnsButton"
                    Content="{DynamicResource gui.columns.button}"
                    ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.columns.hint}"
                    Click="ChooseColumns">
                <Button.ContextMenu>
                    <ContextMenu ItemContainerStyle="{StaticResource ColumnChoiceItem}" />
                </Button.ContextMenu>
            </Button>
        </Grid>

        <!--
            Read only, and it stays that way. Every change to a service goes through a plan,
            so a cell somebody could type into would be a way to change one without ever
            producing a plan.

            Sorting arrived on 2026-08-10 and reordering with S6d2 the day after. What A10 has
            five rules about is answered the same way for both: the list reconciles itself in
            place and holds still while somebody is using it, so a sorted or rearranged list
            does not jump under a hand that is already on it.

            NO COLUMNS ARE DECLARED HERE ANY MORE. Seventeen of them are built in code from
            ViewModels/Columns.cs, because a list somebody chooses from cannot be a list written
            out - and the six that were here would have been a second copy of six of the
            seventeen. ListColumns.cs builds them and the two templated cells live in
            Themes/List.xaml, which is where a column built in code can reach them.
        -->
        <DataGrid Grid.Row="2"
                  x:Name="Entries"
                  MouseEnter="ListEngaged"
                  MouseLeave="ListReleased"
                  GotKeyboardFocus="ListEngaged"
                  LostKeyboardFocus="ListReleased"
                  ItemsSource="{Binding Rows}"
                  Style="{StaticResource ListGrid}"
                  RowStyle="{StaticResource ListRow}"
                  CellStyle="{StaticResource ListCell}"
                  ColumnHeaderStyle="{StaticResource ColumnHeading}"
                  AutoGenerateColumns="False"
                  IsReadOnly="True"
                  CanUserSortColumns="True"
                  CanUserReorderColumns="True"
                  CanUserAddRows="False"
                  GridLinesVisibility="Horizontal"
                  HorizontalGridLinesBrush="{StaticResource SurfaceRowLine}"
                  HeadersVisibility="Column"
                  HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                  SelectionUnit="FullRow"
                  SelectionMode="Single"
                  PreviewMouseRightButtonDown="PointAtRowBeforeMenu"
                  ContextMenuOpening="OfferTheMenuOnlyOnARow">

            <!--
                READ ONLY, LIKE THE LIST. Everything that changes a service goes through a plan,
                so a menu item that stopped one from here would be an operation without a plan -
                rule 1 of the project notes, and not a temporary shortcut either. Those arrive
                with the screen that can show a plan.

                It hangs on the grid rather than on the row style, and that is the difference
                between a menu and a menu that works: a ContextMenu in a resource dictionary has
                nowhere to put a handler. The grid is also an ancestor of whatever cell has focus,
                so the menu key opens it without a second copy for the keyboard.
            -->
            <DataGrid.ContextMenu>
                <ContextMenu>
                    <MenuItem Header="{DynamicResource gui.menu.copyName}" Click="CopyServiceName" />
                    <MenuItem Header="{DynamicResource gui.menu.copyDisplayName}" Click="CopyDisplayName" />
                </ContextMenu>
            </DataGrid.ContextMenu>

        </DataGrid>

        <!--
            WHAT THE MIDDLE OF THE WINDOW SAYS WHEN IT HAS NO ROWS. Complaint 9 of the eleven,
            and the one the line under the list could not answer: an empty rectangle does not
            say whether nothing matched or something broke, and a line of grey text below the
            grid is read after the grid, if at all.

            It sits OVER the grid rather than instead of it, so the column headings stay where
            they are - a list whose headings vanish reads as a window that fell over, and at the
            empty state the rows come back on the next key press. Not hit testable, so the grid
            underneath still takes a click.

            Five states rather than four, and the fifth is why this is worked out in ListState
            rather than here: a machine that hands over nothing is not a query that matched
            nothing, and offering to clear an empty query would be the window being confidently
            wrong.
        -->
        <StackPanel Grid.Row="2"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center"
                    IsHitTestVisible="False"
                    MaxWidth="{StaticResource WidthEmptyState}"
>
            <StackPanel.Style>
                <Style TargetType="StackPanel">
                    <Style.Triggers>
                        <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Says.ListMessage}" Value="">
                            <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                        </DataTrigger>
                    </Style.Triggers>
                </Style>
            </StackPanel.Style>

            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Says.ListMessage}"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       TextAlignment="Center"
                       Style="{StaticResource EmptyStateText}" />

            <!--
                The way out, and it is the only place in this window that says these two keys
                exist. Nielsen's third heuristic - `docs/11` 9.2 names it as this product's
                weakest - and both keys were added the day before and are invisible everywhere
                else. Empty when there is none to offer, which is the loading state and a machine
                with nothing on it.
            -->
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Says.ListWayOut}"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       TextAlignment="Center"
                       Margin="{StaticResource MarginAboveStatus}">
                <TextBlock.Style>
                    <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource SubduedText}">
                        <Style.Triggers>
                            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Says.ListWayOut}" Value="">
                                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                            </DataTrigger>
                        </Style.Triggers>
                    </Style>
                </TextBlock.Style>
            </TextBlock>
        </StackPanel>

        <!--
            Two lines now, and never more than two. The count moved up beside the box it answers
            - complaint 8 - and what is left here is what a result has to ADMIT about itself and
            what is wrong with the question, which are two different things and stay apart.
        -->
        <StackPanel Grid.Row="3" Margin="{StaticResource MarginAboveStatus}">

            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Says.Notice}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
                <TextBlock.Style>
                    <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource NoticeText}">
                        <Style.Triggers>
                            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Says.Notice}" Value="">
                                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                            </DataTrigger>
                        </Style.Triggers>
                    </Style>
                </TextBlock.Style>
            </TextBlock>

            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Says.Problem}" TextWrapping="Wrap">
                <TextBlock.Style>
                    <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource ProblemText}">
                        <Style.Triggers>
                            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Says.Problem}" Value="">
                                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                            </DataTrigger>
                        </Style.Triggers>
                    </Style>
                </TextBlock.Style>
            </TextBlock>
        </StackPanel>
    </Grid>
</Window>
